LENGTH VS TOKEN RELATIONSHIP
======================================================================
Short question: 38 characters -> 9 tokens (4.22 characters/token)
Medium paragraph: 317 characters -> 46 tokens (6.89 characters/token)
Long project document: 1644 characters -> 266 tokens (6.18 characters/token)

Observation:
Text length and token count generally increase together, but they
are not exactly proportional. Tokenizers split text according to
subword patterns rather than simply counting characters or words.
Long or uncommon words, punctuation, code, numbers, and other
languages can therefore produce different token-to-character ratios.





======================================================================
CONCLUSION
======================================================================

The experiment shows that token count generally increases as text
length increases, but the relationship is not exactly proportional.

The three samples produced different characters-per-token ratios,
demonstrating that tokenization depends on the structure and content
of the text rather than character count alone.

The cost calculation also demonstrates why token measurement is
important for a RAG system. Retrieved documents contribute to input
token usage, while generated answers contribute to output token usage.
Therefore, chunk size and retrieval strategy should consider both
context limits and API cost.